---
/* The budget line, drawn twice — for "The nudge that sat below the median".
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The article's whole argument is an ordering, not a quantity: the four marks
   of a session budget (floor, nudge, median closer, ceiling) and the two
   orders they can take. So the drawing is two stacks of the same four marks,
   and the only thing that moves between them is where the nudge sits. No
   value is drawn anywhere — positions are the argument, and the strip above
   the article carries the measured numbers for every window.

   Same idiom as FigureLoop: panes made of `--rule` borders, mono eyebrow
   labels, semantic colour for status only — red for the kill path, green for
   the session that ends on its own terms. */
---

<figure class="fig-budget">
  <div
    class="tracks"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Two orderings of the same four budget marks. Placed off the floor, the nudge sits below the median closing session: it interrupts the typical session, gets ignored, and the ceiling kills the session mid-turn. Placed off the median closer, the nudge only interrupts the outsized session, which wraps up and ends on its own terms."
  >
    <div class="track" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="t-label">the rule that was wrong — nudge placed off the floor</p>
      <ol>
        <li class="mark">
          <span class="m-name">floor</span>
          <span class="m-note">the cheapest session on record</span>
        </li>
        <li class="mark nudge">
          <span class="m-name">nudge</span>
          <span class="m-note">asked to wrap up here — in almost every session</span>
          <span class="m-branch bad">below the median: ridden past, taught to mean nothing</span>
        </li>
        <li class="mark">
          <span class="m-name">median closer</span>
          <span class="m-note">the typical session that finishes something</span>
        </li>
        <li class="mark">
          <span class="m-name">ceiling</span>
          <span class="m-branch bad">a kill mid-turn — commit and handoff never run</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="track" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="t-label">the rule that pays — nudge placed off the median closer</p>
      <ol>
        <li class="mark">
          <span class="m-name">floor</span>
          <span class="m-note">still the smallest thing a session can be</span>
        </li>
        <li class="mark">
          <span class="m-name">median closer</span>
          <span class="m-note">measured in a window with no ceiling in force</span>
        </li>
        <li class="mark nudge">
          <span class="m-name">nudge</span>
          <span class="m-note">clears the median — only the outsized session is asked to stop</span>
          <span class="m-branch good">the session commits, hands off, ends on its own terms</span>
        </li>
        <li class="mark">
          <span class="m-name">ceiling</span>
          <span class="m-note">headroom set from measured wrap-up, not from a ratio</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    The same four marks in the two orders they can take, reading upward from
    the floor. Nothing here is to scale on purpose — the ordering is the whole
    argument, and the measured values for every window are in the strip.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .fig-budget {
    margin: 0;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-l);
    inline-size: min(100%, var(--measure-wide));
  }

  .tracks {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .tracks {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-l);
    }
  }

  .track {
    background: var(--mantle);
    border: var(--rule);
    padding: var(--space-m) var(--space-m) var(--space-s);
  }

  .t-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-m);
  }

  /* The marks read bottom-up like a gauge: the floor at the foot, the ceiling
     at the head. `column-reverse` keeps the YAML-order of the argument (floor
     first) while drawing the budget the way a reader imagines one — more
     tokens is further up. */
  .track ol {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column-reverse;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .mark {
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--base);
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-xs);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  .m-name {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The one accent in each pane: the mark the article is about. */
  .mark.nudge .m-name {
    color: var(--current);
  }

  .m-note {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .m-branch {
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    border-block-start: var(--rule);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  .m-branch.bad {
    color: var(--red);
  }

  .m-branch.good {
    color: var(--green);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
